    "https://jobs.anu.edu.au/jobs/casual-sessional-academic-school-of-computing-canberra-act-act-australia": {
      "title": "Casual Sessional Academic",
      "description": "Casual Sessional Academic - School of Computing - Canberra / ACT, ACT, Australia Privacy Statement People & Culture Information for applicants Salaries & benefits Respect and Inclusion Aboriginal & Torres Strait Islander employment Applicant Login Kiosk mode Casual Sessional Academic - School of Computing Casual Sessional Academic - School of Computing 563679 Canberra / ACT, ACT, Australia, 2601 Casual Closing at: Oct 18 2026 - 23:55 AEDT Add to favourites View favourites Position Description Classification: Casual Sessional Academic Salary package: Per ANU Academic Casual Sessional Rates Terms: Casual About the opportunity As a Casual Sessional Academic (Tutor), you will contribute to the delivery of SOCO courses with support, guidance and direction from the Course Convenor and School staff. You will work both independently and collaboratively to foster active, evidence-based learning, motivate students to achieve their best and support them in meeting course learning outcomes. Applicants should possess qualifications and/or relevant professional experience appropriate to the discipline area. A demonstrated ability to engage and educate students, along with a collaborative approach to teamwork, is highly valued. Tutoring qualifications are highly regarded. If you have not recently completed tutor training offered by the College, you will be required to undertake this professional development. Successful applicants will be advised of the dates, likely around O-Week. Casual Sessional Academic roles at ANU are non-ladder positions and are not eligible for participation in the academic promotions process. About the School The School of Computing has a strong foundation in computing and information sciences at ANU. We are a transformative centre for research in artificial intelligence and machine learning, computer systems and software, and theoretical foundations of computing. We span traditional and modern thinking, connecting decades of computer science methodologies with modern data and computational science. Our mission is motivated by the need to design, drive and sustain strategic activities via five broad focus areas: Computing Foundations, Computational Science, Intelligent Systems, Data Science and Analytics, and the Software Innovation Institute. Be part of an innovative and forward-looking intellectual agenda, built on a diverse, inclusive culture. About the College As one of the great universities of the world, ANU is driven by a culture of excellence in everything we do. Our lived experience is increasingly one of large-scale systems of people, whose actions and interactions are influenced by our digital, physical and biological environment. The ANU College of Systems and Society (CSS) hosts many of the key disciplines necessary to help us design, build, regulate and secure the future. CSS brings together expertise in social, technical, computational, ecological and scientific systems to build a new approach to systems design and build new national capabilities in data-driven policy and business development. The work we do in the College is to strengthen our national mission and meet our unique responsibilities as Australia’s national university. We will deliver on our mission by building a strong community, providing transformative educational experiences, conducting high-impact research, seeking meaningful engagement and becoming a resilient organisation. We welcome and openly acknowledge differences in expertise, research, education, professional focus, experience and perspective. CSS is a vibrant and diverse community of more than three thousand students, staff and visitors. Our College is comprised of the School of Computing, School of Cybernetics, School of Engineering, Fenner School of Environment & Society, Mathematical Sciences Institute and the Australian National Centre for the Public Awareness of Science, supported by the Professional Services Group. Eligibility requirements Applicants for Casual Sessional Academic (CSA) tutoring or demonstrating roles must meet the applicable academic qualification or professional experience equivalence requirements for the course(s) they are seeking to teach. Eligibility will be assessed against the specific course and course level, not simply on whether an applicant is generally qualified to tutor. Applicants may meet the academic eligibility requirement through one or more of the following pathways: Relevant completed qualification: A completed qualification in a relevant discipline at least one AQF level higher than the course being taught. Relevant qualification at the same level: A completed qualification, or equivalent, in a relevant discipline at the same AQF level as the course, together with appropriate professional development and supervision. Exceptional academic achievement: For eligible 1XXX–3XXX and 6XXX–7XXX level courses, demonstrated achievement of the relevant learning outcomes to an exceptional level, normally evidenced by a High Distinction (HD) in the relevant course or equivalent study, as determined by the School. Current doctoral candidature: Current PhD/doctoral candidates may be eligible where the proposed teaching is within or sufficiently related to their area of doctoral study. Relevant professional registration and practice: Current registration in an applicable profession together with current practice relevant to the proposed teaching. Where professional registration and practice form the basis for professional equivalency, a separate, more detailed assessment process will be required. Applicants will be contacted separately if additional information is needed. Please ensure your application clearly identifies, where relevant: These questions can be answered on the Course Preference/Eligibility Form but may also be included in your formal application documents. https://forms.cloud.microsoft/r/2u7rynUycb?origin=lprLink your completed qualifications, including discipline/field, institution and year awarded; your current study, including whether you are an undergraduate, postgraduate coursework or doctoral student; if you are a doctoral candidate, your area of research/thesis topic; any relevant courses in which you achieved an HD, particularly courses you are applying to tutor or closely related courses; your previous tutoring/demonstrating experience, including the course(s) and when you most recently taught them; any relevant professional registration, professional practice or industry experience; and any other academic or professional experience that demonstrates knowledge of the subject matter relevant to your preferred courses. Where information cannot be verified through ANU records, you may be asked to provide supporting evidence. Additional course-specific eligibility requirements Some teaching allocations are subject to additional restrictions: Undergraduate coursework students may only independently tutor/demonstrate undergraduate 1XXX–3XXX level course instances. Where undergraduate and postgraduate students are taught together, an undergraduate tutor may only participate under an approved team-teaching arrangement, as determined by the Course Convenor. Postgraduate coursework students who have achieved an HD in the relevant postgraduate course may be eligible to tutor the postgraduate and/or corresponding undergraduate instance. Undergraduate and postgraduate coursework students are not ordinarily permitted to tutor 4XXX or 8XXX-level courses, except where exceptional approval is granted by the Associate Director (Education). A CSA will not ordinarily be selected to teach more than two courses in a semester or teaching session. Returning tutors If you have previously been approved and have taught the same course, an existing professional experience equivalence assessment may be reused where: the most recent approved assessment was completed within the previous two years; you have actively tutored or demonstrated in the same course within the previous 12 months; and there has been no material change affecting the basis of the previous assessment. Meeting the academic qualification or professional experience equivalence requirements establishes eligibility for consideration only. It does not guarantee selection or appointment. Previous teaching performance, professional conduct, reliability and suitability for the proposed teaching role may also be taken into account. Selection process Appointment to these roles will occur through a three-step process: Step 1: Application Assessment All submitted applications will be reviewed against the selection criteria, including submitted documentation such as the statement of suitability, CV and completed Course Preference/Eligibility Form. Step 2: Equivalency Assessment (AQF Requirements) Applicants who meet the requirements of Step 1 will be automatically assessed against the relevant Professional Experience Equivalency requirements based on the information provided in their completed Course Preference/Eligibility Form. https://forms.cloud.microsoft/r/2u7rynUycb?origin=lprLink If an assessment cannot be made from this information, you may be asked to complete the Professional Experience Equivalency Form and/or provide additional information to enable assessment against the Australian Qualifications Framework (AQF) requirements relevant to the course(s) you wish to tutor. Further information is available in the relevant ANU Professional Experience Equivalency policy. Step 3: Final Appointment Requirements Successful candidates must complete all required HR onboarding documentation and any mandatory tutor training prior to commencing teaching duties. Important information Review of applications will begin after the closing date and continue until all positions are filled. Preparation for Semester 1, 2027 teaching begins in O-Week from 15 February 2027, with some courses potentially requiring earlier preparation. Completion of compulsory training may be required in the weeks leading up to Semester 1, 2027 if you have not completed the required training previously or your training is not up to date. Applications received late or missing required documentation may not be considered. Availability of tutor hours for any course is subject to change depending on enrolment numbers. An offer of a CSA contract does not guarantee hours will be available. The School of Computing intends to collect tutor performance feedback from Course Convenors in 2026. This feedback may be shared with future Convenors for subsequent teaching opportunities. Further details will be provided to successful applicants. How to apply Applications must be submitted via the ANU recruitment portal and include the following: Curriculum Vitae (CV): Including contact details for at least two referees, preferably a current or previous supervisor. Statement – maximum 2 pages: Your statement should include: a summary of teaching experience at ANU or elsewhere, including relevant industry experience; courses previously tutored, if applicable; a brief statement outlining your approach to tutoring; availability for teaching from 15 February–30 June 2027; and interview availability in November 2026. Course Preference/Eligibility Form: Applicants must complete the Course Preference/Eligibility Form and select three course preferences only via the provided link. https://forms.cloud.microsoft/r/2u7rynUycb?origin=lprLink Please note A formal offer of employment will only be issued by HR once all stages of the selection and eligibility assessment process have been successfully completed. A CSA will not ordinarily be selected to tutor more than two courses in a semester or teaching session. Availability of tutor hours for any course is subject to change depending on enrolment numbers. An offer of a CSA contract does not guarantee hours will be available. Applications which do not address the selection criteria or provide the required documentation may not be considered for the position. The School of Computing intends to collect performance tutor feedback from Course Convenors in 2026. This feedback may be shared with future Convenors for subsequent teaching opportunities. Further details will be provided to successful applicants. Enquiries To enquire about this role, please contact: Associate Director of Education                          SOCO Admin Team adir.education.comp@anu.edu.au admin.comp@anu.edu.au Applications which do not address the selection criteria or provide the required paperwork will not be considered for the position. Please note: The preferred candidate must have rights to live and work in Australia and will be required to complete mandatory pre-employment declarations and background checks relevant to the role they have applied for, in accordance with the Background Checking Procedure and the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-Based Violence . Apply Now First Name (required) df205e53 Last Name (required) cccd45ce Email (required) 146ef2f8 Refer someone to this job Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Job referral Know someone great for this role! Recommended Jobs Executive Support Officer Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Join the Office of the Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Education) to play a key role in supporting senior leadership in a dynamic and complex environment. Senior Advisor Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Provide strategic advice and coordination to the Interim Vice-Chancellor, delivering high-level briefings and supporting University priorities. University Executive Committee Secretary Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Provide high-level governance and secretariat support to the University Executive Committee, working closely with senior University leaders. Research Officer Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 NCEPH is seeking an experienced Research Officer to provide data collection and project support for a Medical Research Future Fund (MRFF) project on the design and delivery of psychosocial support services. Employability and Engagement Systems Coordinator Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Provide frontline support for careers and placement systems, assist staff and students, and deliver data insights to support KPIs, reporting, and employability priorities. International Partnerships and Agreements Coordinator Canberra / ACT, ACT, 2601 Support global partnerships by coordinating international agreements, managing compliance and risk, and engaging key stakeholders. Job Alerts First Name (required) 69080035 Last Name (required) d5608d95 Email (required) f123f92c Job Alert afb9a98d Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Departments School of Computing School of Computing Categories Academic Academic Employment Type Casual Casual Locations Canberra / ACT, ACT, Australia Canberra / ACT, ACT, Australia Classification Casual/Sessional Academic Casual/Sessional Academic Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Back to top Acknowledgement of Country The Australian National University acknowledges, celebrates and pays our respects to the Ngunnawal and Ngambri people of the Canberra region and to all First Nations Australians on whose traditional lands we meet and work, and whose cultures are among the oldest continuing cultures in human history. Contact ANU Copyright Disclaimer Privacy Freedom of Information Accessibility +61 2 6125 5111 The Australian National University, Canberra TEQSA Provider ID: PRV12002 (Australian University) CRICOS Provider Code: 00120C ABN: 52 234 063 906 Candidate Details This website uses cookies. We use cookies to personalise content such as job recommendations, and to analyse our traffic. You consent to our cookies if you click \"I Accept\". If you click on \"I Do Not Accept\", then we will not use cookies but you may have a deteriorated user experience. You can change your settings by clicking on the Settings link on the top right of the device I do not accept I accept Please choose a classification"
